/* Mini-lessons: Security */
Object.assign(LESSONS, {
'quiz:sec:CIA triad': {
  why: `The CIA triad is the vocabulary of security. Interviewers use it to check that you think about what is actually at risk.`,
  idea: `<ul class="clean"><li><b>Confidentiality</b>: only the right people can see the data (encryption, access control).</li><li><b>Integrity</b>: the data is accurate and unchanged (hashes, permissions, audit logs).</li><li><b>Availability</b>: systems work when needed (backups, redundancy, monitoring).</li></ul>`,
  example: `<p>A lost unencrypted laptop with patient files hurts <b>confidentiality</b>. A changed invoice amount hurts <b>integrity</b>. A POS outage during dinner hurts <b>availability</b>.</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Calling every incident a confidentiality problem.`, `Forgetting availability is security too; an outage is a security concern.`],
  say: `I look at which part of CIA is at risk; that tells me whether the fix is about access, accuracy or uptime.`
},
'quiz:sec:Authentication & MFA': {
  why: `Most account compromises start with a stolen password. MFA blocks most of them, and support techs help users set it up every day.`,
  idea: `<ul class="clean"><li><b>Authentication</b> = proving who you are. <b>Authorization</b> = what you're allowed to do.</li><li>Factor types: something you <b>know</b> (password, PIN), <b>have</b> (authenticator app, security key), <b>are</b> (fingerprint, face).</li><li>MFA = two <b>different</b> types. A password plus a PIN is still one type.</li></ul>`,
  example: `<p>Password + code from an authenticator app = know + have = real MFA. Password + security question = know + know = not MFA.</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Thinking two passwords are MFA.`, `Turning MFA off to fix a user's lockout instead of fixing the cause.`],
  say: `MFA combines two different factor types, like a password and an authenticator app, which stops most stolen-password attacks.`
},
'quiz:sec:Cryptography': {
  why: `Knowing when to hash and when to encrypt comes up in security interviews and secure-coding reviews.`,
  idea: `<ul class="clean"><li><b>Hashing</b> is one-way: used to check integrity and store passwords (SHA-256; for passwords bcrypt or Argon2). MD5 and SHA-1 are outdated.</li><li><b>Symmetric encryption</b>: one shared key, fast (AES, ChaCha20).</li><li><b>Asymmetric encryption</b>: a public/private key pair (RSA, ECC), used for key exchange and digital signatures, as in HTTPS.</li></ul>`,
  example: `<p>Storing user passwords: don't encrypt them, <b>hash</b> them with a slow, salted algorithm like bcrypt. Protecting a file you'll need to read later: <b>encrypt</b> it with AES.</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Calling Base64 "encryption." It's just encoding, anyone can reverse it.`, `Using fast hashes like plain SHA-256 for passwords.`],
  say: `Hashing is one-way for integrity and passwords, encryption is two-way for confidentiality; HTTPS uses asymmetric crypto to agree on a symmetric key.`
},
'quiz:sec:Windows logs': {
  why: `SOC analysts and sysadmins read Windows Security logs to investigate sign-in problems and suspicious activity.`,
  idea: `<ul class="clean"><li><b>4624</b> successful logon · <b>4625</b> failed logon · <b>4634</b> logoff</li><li><b>4740</b> account locked out · <b>4720</b> user created · <b>4726</b> user deleted</li><li><b>4672</b> logon with special (admin) privileges</li></ul><p>Find them in Event Viewer → Windows Logs → Security, or in a SIEM.</p>`,
  example: `<p>A user keeps getting locked out. Filter for <b>4740</b> to see when; the event shows the caller computer. Then look at <b>4625</b> failures from that machine, often an old saved password on a phone or mapped drive.</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Mixing up 4624 (success) and 4625 (failure).`, `Looking at one event alone instead of the pattern over time.`],
  say: `For lockouts I filter 4740 to find the source, then the 4625 failures from that device to find the stale credential.`
},
'quiz:sec:Vulnerabilities': {
  why: `Security teams can't patch everything at once. CVSS scores and exposure decide what gets fixed first.`,
  idea: `<ul class="clean"><li>A <b>CVE</b> is a publicly known vulnerability; <b>CVSS</b> scores its severity from 0 to 10.</li><li>Ratings: 0.1–3.9 Low · 4.0–6.9 Medium · 7.0–8.9 High · 9.0–10.0 Critical.</li><li>Real priority also depends on exposure (internet-facing?) and whether it's being actively exploited.</li></ul>`,
  example: `<p>Two findings: CVSS 9.8 on an internal test server, and CVSS 7.5 on the public website with known active exploitation. The public website likely goes first: high severity plus exposure plus active exploitation.</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Sorting purely by score and ignoring exposure.`, `Assuming "Medium" means safe to ignore.`],
  say: `I start from the CVSS rating, then adjust for exposure and active exploitation to decide what to patch first.`
},
'quiz:sec:Backups': {
  why: `Backups are the last line of defense against ransomware, mistakes and hardware failure. Untested backups are a classic hidden risk.`,
  idea: `<ul class="clean"><li><b>3-2-1 rule</b>: 3 copies of the data, on 2 different types of storage, with 1 copy offsite.</li><li>Keep one copy <b>offline or immutable</b> so ransomware can't encrypt it.</li><li><b>Test restores</b> regularly. A backup you can't restore isn't a backup.</li></ul>`,
  example: `<p>A plan with the live data plus one copy on a NAS in the same office has 2 copies, 1 location: it fails 3-2-1. Adding a cloud backup gives 3 copies, 2 storage types, 1 offsite.</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Keeping every copy in the same building.`, `Never testing a restore.`],
  say: `I follow 3-2-1 with one immutable copy, and I test restores, because a backup only counts if it comes back.`
},
'quiz:sec:Secrets & untrusted code': {
  why: `Leaked keys and scripts nobody reviewed are two of the most common ways in. SOC and support roles deal with both.`,
  idea: `<ul class="clean"><li><b>Secrets never live in the code.</b> Keep them in environment variables or a secrets manager, outside the repo.</li><li><b>Catch them before commit</b> with a pre-commit or preflight scan. Git history keeps everything.</li><li><b>Exposed? Revoke and rotate first.</b> Deleting the line doesn't help; someone may already have it.</li><li><b>Untrusted code is read, not run</b>, until it's been reviewed. Quarantine it.</li></ul>`,
  example: `<p>A developer pastes an API key into a config file and pushes it. The pre-commit scan should have stopped it; since it didn't, the key is revoked and a new one issued first, then the file is fixed and the key is moved to a secrets manager. A script downloaded from a forum is read and reviewed in a sandbox before anyone runs it.</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Deleting a leaked key from the file but never revoking it.`, `Running a downloaded script "to see what it does."`],
  say: `I keep secrets out of the repo, scan before every commit, and if one leaks I revoke and rotate it first. Code I didn't write gets read and reviewed before it ever runs.`
},
'quiz:sec:Least privilege & access control': {
  why: `Every breach review asks "why did that account have that access?" Giving just enough access, and checking it, is daily work in IT support, cloud and security roles.`,
  idea: `<ul class="clean"><li><b>Least privilege</b>: each user, service and script gets only the access its job needs, only for as long as it needs it.</li><li><b>Separation of duties</b>: split risky work so one person can't do it alone (one person adds a vendor, another approves its payments).</li><li>Access control models: <b>RBAC</b> (role-based access control) gives permissions by job role; <b>ABAC</b> (attribute-based access control) uses attributes like department, device or time; <b>DAC</b> (discretionary access control) lets the owner decide who can use a file; <b>MAC</b> (mandatory access control) uses labels set by policy that users can't change.</li><li>Review access regularly and remove it when people change roles, or access piles up ("privilege creep").</li><li><b>Accountability</b>: everyone uses their own account and actions are logged, so you can tell who did what. Shared logins break it.</li><li><b>Defense in depth</b>: layer controls (MFA, endpoint protection, firewalls, backups) so one failure isn't fatal.</li></ul>`,
  seen: `<pre>Group: Finance-Approvers   Permission: approve payments over $10,000
Members: j.lee, m.ortiz, temp.contractor7   (contract ended in March)</pre><p>An access review export: a leftover member still sits in a powerful group.</p>`,
  example: `<p>A ticket says: give the new marketing hire "the same access as Sam." Sam has been here six years and collected access to finance shares along the way. Copying Sam grants far more than the job needs. Instead, add the hire to the Marketing role group, which carries exactly what marketing needs.</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Copying another user's access ("make them like Sam").`, `Making everyone a local admin to stop the tickets.`, `Never removing access after someone changes roles.`],
  spot: {q: `A developer keeps permanent admin rights on production servers "in case of emergencies." What's the problem?`, a: `Standing admin rights break least privilege and make that account a prime target. Use just-in-time access instead: elevation that is requested, approved, time-limited and logged.`},
  say: `I grant access by role instead of copying other users, keep admin rights temporary, and review group memberships so access matches the job.`
},
'quiz:sec:Attack types': {
  why: `Recognizing an attack from its symptoms is the first skill in a SOC (security operations center) or help desk role. It tells you what to do first and who to call.`,
  idea: `<ul class="clean"><li><b>Social engineering</b> tricks people: phishing (email), smishing (text message), vishing (voice call), pretexting (a made-up story to get information).</li><li><b>Password attacks</b>: brute force (many passwords against one account), password spraying (one common password against many accounts), credential stuffing (username and password pairs leaked from another site, tried here).</li><li><b>Malware</b>: ransomware encrypts files and demands payment; a trojan pretends to be useful software.</li><li><b>Network and web</b>: a DDoS (distributed denial of service) floods a service from many machines; an on-path attacker (man-in-the-middle) intercepts traffic; SQL injection and XSS (cross-site scripting) abuse input the application didn't check.</li><li>Each has a matching defense: MFA, user training, patching, parameterized queries and output encoding, rate limiting, tested backups.</li><li><b>Telling spraying from stuffing in real logs</b>: logs rarely show the password tried. Stuffing usually hits many usernames that don't exist here, gets a few successes, and comes from many IP addresses; spraying uses real usernames, and identity providers often flag it for you.</li></ul>`,
  seen: `<pre>08:02:11 FAILED login user=alice src=203.0.113.50
08:02:12 FAILED login user=bob   src=203.0.113.50
08:02:13 FAILED login user=chen  src=203.0.113.50
08:02:14 FAILED login user=dana  src=203.0.113.50</pre><p>One source, many accounts, one try each: password spraying <i>or</i> credential stuffing. The password used (same one, or each account's own) tells them apart.</p>`,
  example: `<p>A user's account signs in from another country minutes after their password showed up in a public breach of a shopping site they reused it on. The same addresses also made single failed attempts on dozens of other accounts, each with a different password. That's credential stuffing: reset the affected passwords, make sure MFA is enforced, and block known-breached passwords.</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Looking only at one account's lockouts: spraying stays under per-account limits, so count accounts per source instead.`, `Calling a suspicious email "spam" and deleting it instead of reporting it as phishing.`, `Assuming MFA makes password attacks irrelevant. Still investigate, and watch for repeated MFA prompts the user didn't start.`],
  spot: {q: `200 failed logins hit 200 different accounts, one attempt each, all with the password "Spring2026!". The lockout policy never triggered. What is it?`, a: `Password spraying: one common password against many accounts, kept under the lockout limit. Detect it by counting distinct accounts per source, not failures per account.`},
  say: `I identify the attack from its pattern, like the same password tried across many accounts for spraying, apply the matching defense, and escalate with the evidence.`
},
'quiz:sec:Firewalls': {
  why: `Many "it can't connect" tickets end at a firewall rule, and cloud network filters use the same ideas. Reading rules correctly matters for NOC (network operations center), Linux and cloud roles.`,
  idea: `<ul class="clean"><li>A firewall allows or blocks traffic by rules: source, destination, port and protocol.</li><li>Rules are checked <b>top to bottom and the first match wins</b>. Rule sets end with a deny-all, and good practice assumes an implicit deny: anything not allowed is blocked. (Not every product defaults this way, so check.)</li><li>In the cloud: Amazon Web Services (AWS) network ACLs (access control lists) use numbered, first-match rules ending in a deny; AWS security groups are allow-only and stateful, with no rule order (anything not allowed is dropped).</li><li><b>Stateful</b> firewalls track connections, so replies to allowed traffic come back in automatically. <b>Stateless</b> firewalls (packet filters) judge each packet alone, so return traffic needs its own rule.</li><li>Put specific rules above broad ones, and open only the ports and sources a service needs.</li></ul>`,
  seen: `<pre>#  Action  Source          Port
1  allow   10.0.5.0/24     22
2  deny    any             22
3  allow   any             443
4  deny    any             any</pre><p>The admin subnet can use SSH, everyone else is blocked from it, and HTTPS is open to all.</p>`,
  example: `<p>Using the table above: a packet from 10.0.5.20 to port 22 matches rule 1 and is allowed. The same packet from 198.51.100.7 isn't in 10.0.5.0/24, so it falls to rule 2 and is denied. Swap rules 1 and 2 and the deny comes first, blocking the admins too.</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Adding a broad allow above a specific deny, so the deny never runs.`, `Opening a port to any source "just to test" and forgetting to close it.`, `Forgetting that a stateless filter needs a rule for return traffic.`],
  spot: {q: `Rule 1: allow any source to port 3389. Rule 2: deny 0.0.0.0/0 to port 3389. Is Remote Desktop blocked from the internet?`, a: `No. Rule 1 matches first and allows everyone, so rule 2 never runs. Limit rule 1 to a trusted range, or require a VPN, and keep the deny below it.`},
  say: `I read rules top to bottom with first match wins, keep specific rules above broad ones, and open only the ports and sources a service needs.`
},
'quiz:sec:Incident response': {
  why: `When something goes wrong, a calm, ordered process limits the damage. SOC (security operations center) and IT interviews often ask you to walk through it.`,
  idea: `<ol><li><b>Preparation</b>: plans, contacts, logging and tools ready before anything happens.</li><li><b>Detection</b>: noticing something is wrong (an alert, a user report).</li><li><b>Analysis</b>: confirming it's real, finding the scope, setting severity.</li><li><b>Containment</b>: stopping the spread (isolate the device, disable the account).</li><li><b>Eradication</b>: removing the cause (malware, attacker access, the weakness used).</li><li><b>Recovery</b>: restoring from clean backups and watching for a return.</li><li><b>Lessons learned</b>: what happened and what to change; update the plan.</li></ol><p class="small">These seven steps are how CompTIA Security+ splits NIST's classic four-phase lifecycle: Preparation; Detection and Analysis; Containment, Eradication and Recovery; Post-incident activity (lessons learned). NIST's latest guidance (Special Publication (SP) 800-61 Rev. 3, April 2025) arranges the same work around the NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF) 2.0 functions.</p>`,
  seen: `<pre>ALERT  EDR  High  laptop-HR-07  Ransomware behavior: 312 files renamed *.locked in 2 minutes</pre><p>An EDR (endpoint detection and response) alert: detection has happened, so analysis and containment come next.</p>`,
  example: `<p>For the alert above: confirm it's real and check other machines (analysis), isolate laptop-HR-07 from the network in the EDR console (containment), remove the malware and the way it got in (eradication), restore the files from a clean backup (recovery), then write up how it arrived and what would have stopped it (lessons learned).</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Wiping the machine before collecting evidence.`, `Restoring before the cause is removed, so the attacker comes straight back.`, `Skipping lessons learned, so the same incident happens again.`],
  spot: {q: `A tech finds ransomware on a laptop and immediately restores the files from backup while the laptop is still on the network. What's missing?`, a: `Containment and eradication. Isolate the laptop and remove the cause first; restoring onto an infected, connected machine just gets the files encrypted again.`},
  say: `I confirm and scope the incident, contain it first, remove the cause, recover from clean backups, then capture lessons learned so it doesn't repeat.`
},
'quiz:sec:SSH hardening': {
  why: `Every internet-facing Linux server gets password-guessing traffic on SSH within minutes of going online. Locking SSH down is one of the first jobs on any new server, and a classic interview question.`,
  idea: `<ul class="clean"><li>The usual baseline in <code>/etc/ssh/sshd_config</code>: <code>PasswordAuthentication no</code> (keys only), <code>PermitRootLogin no</code> (sign in as yourself, then use sudo), a short <code>AllowUsers</code> list, and a low <code>MaxAuthTries</code>.</li><li><b>The first value wins.</b> sshd keeps the first value it reads for each setting. On Ubuntu, <code>sshd_config</code> starts with <code>Include /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/*.conf</code>, so those files (in name order) beat anything later in the main file. Cloud images often ship <code>50-cloud-init.conf</code> with passwords switched on.</li><li>Key files must stay private: sshd ignores <code>authorized_keys</code> if group or others can write it, its <code>~/.ssh</code> folder or the home folder. <code>~/.ssh</code> at 700 and <code>authorized_keys</code> at 600 is the safe standard.</li><li>Passwords have a second path: <code>KbdInteractiveAuthentication</code> (keyboard-interactive, often through PAM (pluggable authentication modules)) must be off too.</li><li>Change safely: check the config with <code>sshd -t</code>, keep your current session open, and test a new sign-in before logging out.</li></ul>`,
  seen: `<pre>$ grep -ri passwordauthentication /etc/ssh/sshd_config /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/
/etc/ssh/sshd_config:PasswordAuthentication no
/etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/50-cloud-init.conf:PasswordAuthentication yes</pre><p>The main file says no, but the drop-in is read first, so passwords are still allowed.</p>`,
  example: `<p>Deploy's key sign-in stopped working, so someone switched passwords back on. The real cause: <code>authorized_keys</code> had been made writable by everyone, so sshd ignored it. The fix: back to the standard, <code>chmod 700 ~/.ssh</code> and <code>chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys</code>, then turn passwords off in the drop-in that actually wins. Now it's secure, and deploy can still sign in.</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Editing only the main sshd_config while a drop-in that is read earlier says the opposite.`, `Turning off passwords before checking that key sign-in works, and locking everyone out.`, `Fixing a broken key sign-in by re-enabling passwords instead of fixing the file permissions.`],
  spot: {q: `An admin sets PasswordAuthentication no at the bottom of sshd_config and reloads, but password sign-ins still work. Why?`, a: `A file in sshd_config.d (included at the top) already set PasswordAuthentication yes, and sshd keeps the first value it reads. Change it in that drop-in, or add an earlier-named drop-in.`},
  say: `I harden SSH with keys only, no root login and an allow-list, I check which file's value actually wins because sshd keeps the first one, and I test a new sign-in before closing my session.`
}
});

/* Security+ domain 5 (security program management and oversight): governance, risk, third parties, compliance and audits,
   awareness. Terms follow CompTIA's SY0-701 and SY0-801 objectives (see OBJECTIVES in exam.js). */
Object.assign(LESSONS, {
'quiz:sec:Governance & policy': {
  why: `Security+ gives this whole area 20% of SY0-701 and 14% of SY0-801 (the version launching in November 2026), and every IT job runs inside it: you follow the procedures, point users to the policy, and know whose decision a data question is.`,
  idea: `<ul class="clean"><li><b>Policy</b>: management's high-level statement of what must happen and why, such as the acceptable use policy (AUP), the information security policy or the incident response policy.</li><li><b>Standard</b>: a specific, mandatory, measurable rule that makes a policy concrete: minimum password length, which encryption to use.</li><li><b>Procedure</b>: the step-by-step how-to: onboarding and offboarding, change management, incident playbooks.</li><li><b>Guideline</b>: recommended advice. Helpful, but optional.</li><li>Laws, regulations and industry rules (local, national and global) shape all of these, and the documents are monitored and revised. Boards and committees set direction; governance can be centralized or decentralized.</li><li><b>Roles for data</b>: the <b>data owner</b> is the senior person accountable for it (classifies it, approves access); the <b>custodian</b> looks after it day to day (backups, storage, applying the owner's rules), and a <b>steward</b> looks after its quality and proper use; the <b>controller</b> is the organization that decides why and how personal data is processed; the <b>processor</b> processes it for the controller, only on its instructions.</li></ul>`,
  seen: `<pre>POL-07  Acceptable Use Policy     approved by the board, 2026-03
STD-12  Password Standard         min length 14; breached-password check; MFA for admins
PRO-31  Offboarding Procedure     1 disable account  2 collect laptop + badge  3 move files
GDL-04  Secure Coding Guideline   recommended practices; teams may adapt</pre><p>One document library, four kinds of document: the prefix tells you how binding each one is.</p>`,
  example: `<p>A new hire asks whether they may install a browser extension on their work laptop. The <b>policy</b> (the AUP) says company devices run approved software only; the <b>standard</b> lists what is approved; the <b>procedure</b> says how to request something new; a <b>guideline</b> may suggest safe extensions for personal devices. Each document answers a different question.</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Calling a mandatory, measurable rule ("at least 14 characters") a policy: that's a standard.`, `Treating a guideline as mandatory, or a standard as optional.`, `Mixing up owner and custodian: the owner decides, the custodian carries it out.`, `Thinking a processor may use the data for its own purposes: it acts only on the controller's instructions.`],
  spot: {q: `A payroll vendor starts using a client's employee list to market its own pension products. What's wrong?`, a: `The vendor is a data processor: it may use the data only as the client (the controller) instructs. Using it for its own marketing breaks the contract and privacy law such as the GDPR (General Data Protection Regulation).`},
  say: `I separate what must happen (policy) from the exact rule (standard), the steps (procedure) and the advice (guideline), and I know who owns each data set and who only looks after it.`
},
'quiz:sec:Risk management': {
  why: `Every security decision is a risk decision: what could go wrong, how likely, how bad, and what's worth spending to stop it. Both versions of Security+ test the vocabulary and the loss arithmetic.`,
  idea: `<ul class="clean"><li>The process: identify risks, assess them (once, on a schedule or continuously), analyze them, record them in a <b>risk register</b> (risk, owner, likelihood, impact, treatment), and report on them.</li><li><b>Qualitative</b> analysis rates likelihood and impact as High, Medium or Low (a heat map). <b>Quantitative</b> analysis uses money: SLE (single loss expectancy) = asset value × EF (exposure factor, the share lost in one incident), and ALE (annualized loss expectancy) = SLE × ARO (annualized rate of occurrence: once every 4 years is 0.25).</li><li>Four treatments: <b>mitigate</b> (controls lower the likelihood or impact), <b>transfer</b> (insurance or a contract moves the cost; you stay accountable), <b>avoid</b> (stop the risky activity), <b>accept</b> (a documented, approved decision; exceptions and exemptions are formal acceptances).</li><li><b>Risk appetite</b> is how much risk leadership will take on to reach its goals. What is left after controls is residual risk.</li><li>A BIA (business impact analysis) finds the critical functions and sets the RTO (recovery time objective: the target time to restore a system after an outage) and the RPO (recovery point objective: the most data, in time, you can lose). MTTR (mean time to repair) and MTBF (mean time between failures) measure reliability.</li></ul>`,
  seen: `<pre>ID    Risk                        Owner        Likelihood  Impact  ALE       Treatment
R-04  Ransomware on file server   IT manager   Medium      High    $45,000   Mitigate: endpoint detection (EDR) + offline backups
R-09  Laptop theft                Facilities   High        Low     $24,000   Transfer: insurance; mitigate: disk encryption
R-12  Legacy kiosk can't patch    Ops lead     Low         Low     $800      Accept: exception to 2027-06</pre><p>A risk register: each risk has an owner, a rating, a yearly loss estimate and a decision.</p>`,
  example: `<p>Risk R-09 above: laptops are worth $2,000 each and a theft loses the whole laptop (EF 100%), so SLE = $2,000. The company loses about 12 a year (ARO 12), so ALE = $24,000. Insurance transfers the hardware cost and disk encryption protects the data on a stolen laptop. If a control would cost more each year than the loss it prevents, accepting can be the right call, as long as it is documented and approved.</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Multiplying the full asset value by the ARO: ALE = SLE × ARO.`, `Writing an ARO of 4 for "once every 4 years": it's 1/4 = 0.25.`, `Thinking transfer removes accountability: after a breach, the organization still answers for it.`, `Mixing up RTO and RPO: RTO is the time to restore, RPO is the data you can lose.`, `Accepting a risk silently: acceptance must be documented and approved.`],
  spot: {q: `Backups of the order database run every night at 01:00. The business sets an RPO of 4 hours. Is that met?`, a: `No. A failure at 00:59 loses almost 24 hours of orders. Backups (or replication) must run at least every 4 hours to meet the RPO.`},
  say: `I put a money figure on risk where I can (SLE × ARO = ALE), choose to mitigate, transfer, avoid or accept with a documented decision, and keep it all in the risk register with an owner.`
},
'quiz:sec:Third-party risk': {
  why: `Most companies run on vendors: cloud hosting, payroll, managed IT. A vendor's breach becomes your breach, so Security+ asks how you choose them, write the contract and keep checking.`,
  idea: `<ul class="clean"><li><b>Selection</b>: due diligence (finances, breach history, certifications, references), and checking that whoever chooses has no conflict of interest.</li><li><b>Assessment and monitoring</b>: security questionnaires, evidence of the vendor's own audits, independent assessments, penetration testing with the vendor's permission, and a <b>right-to-audit clause</b> so you can check later. Monitoring continues after signing.</li><li><b>Agreements</b>: SLA (service-level agreement: measurable targets such as uptime and response times, usually with penalties); MOU (memorandum of understanding: shared intent, usually not legally binding); MOA (memorandum of agreement: more formal, sets out each side's responsibilities); NDA (non-disclosure agreement: keep information confidential); MSA (master service agreement: the general terms for all future work); SOW (statement of work: one project's deliverables, timeline and price), also called a WO (work order).</li><li><b>Rules of engagement</b> set what a tester or vendor may do, when, and how to stop if something goes wrong.</li></ul>`,
  seen: `<pre>Vendor review: CloudPay Ltd (payroll)        status: APPROVED WITH CONDITIONS
Due diligence   SOC 2 audit report 2026 ✓   breach history: none found ✓
Questionnaire   MFA on admin ✓   encryption at rest ✓   subprocessor list ✗ (requested)
Contract        MSA signed; SOW-3 payroll migration; SLA 99.9%; right to audit ✓; NDA ✓
Next review     2027-05 (yearly)</pre><p>A vendor file (a System and Organization Controls (SOC) 2 report is an independent auditor's report on a service company's controls): what was checked before signing, what the contract guarantees, and when it gets checked again.</p>`,
  example: `<p>Choosing a payroll provider: run due diligence and send a security questionnaire; sign an MSA for the general terms and a SOW for the migration project; put uptime and support targets in the SLA; add a right-to-audit clause; and have the consultant who sees your HR data sign an NDA. Then review the vendor every year, not just once.</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Trusting a questionnaire alone: ask for evidence, such as independent audit reports.`, `Confusing an MSA (general terms) with a SOW (one project).`, `Treating an MOU as a binding contract.`, `Stopping the checks once the contract is signed.`],
  spot: {q: `A contract with a cloud backup vendor promises "best effort" availability and has no audit clause. What's missing?`, a: `Measurable SLA targets (for example 99.9% uptime and a maximum restore time, with penalties) and a right-to-audit clause, so you can verify the vendor's controls later.`},
  say: `I vet vendors before signing, put measurable targets and audit rights in the contract, and keep checking them, because their breach becomes ours.`
},
'quiz:sec:Compliance & audits': {
  why: `Regulators, customers and card brands all want proof that controls work. Security+ asks what non-compliance costs, how privacy rights work, and what each kind of audit and test is for.`,
  idea: `<ul class="clean"><li><b>Consequences of non-compliance</b>: fines, sanctions, reputational damage, loss of license, contractual impacts.</li><li><b>Compliance monitoring</b>: due diligence and due care, attestation (a formal statement that controls are in place), acknowledgement (staff confirm they've read a policy), internal and external reporting, and automation.</li><li><b>Privacy</b>: the data subject is the person the data is about. The controller decides why and how it is processed; the processor acts on the controller's instructions. The <b>right to be forgotten</b> lets people ask for their data to be erased, and data retention rules keep data only as long as needed or legally required.</li><li><b>Audits</b>: internal (the organization's own auditors, often reporting to an audit committee, plus self-assessments) or external (regulators' examinations, independent third-party audits).</li><li><b>Penetration tests</b> differ by how much the testers are told: known, partially known or unknown environment. Reconnaissance is <b>passive</b> (public information, no contact with the target's systems) or <b>active</b> (scanning and probing them).</li></ul>`,
  seen: `<pre>Finding 2026-14    source: external audit (independent firm)
Control            Quarterly access review for finance systems
Evidence           Q1 ✓   Q2 ✓   Q3 missing
Rating             Significant deficiency
Action owner       Finance IT lead        due 2026-12-15</pre><p>An external audit finding: the control exists on paper, but there is no evidence it ran in one quarter.</p>`,
  example: `<p>A shop must keep invoices for 7 years, and a former customer asks to be forgotten. The shop erases their marketing profile, keeps only the records the law requires, and tells the customer why. Before the yearly external audit, the internal audit team runs the same checks, so problems get fixed first.</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Thinking an internal audit is worthless because it isn't independent: it finds problems before the regulator does.`, `Calling a port scan passive reconnaissance: anything that touches the target's systems is active.`, `Assuming an erasure request overrides every law: records the law requires can be kept, and the person should be told.`, `Confusing a known environment (testers told everything) with an unknown one (told nothing).`],
  spot: {q: `A tester is hired for an "unknown environment" test, but on day one the client emails them network diagrams and admin passwords. What's wrong?`, a: `With diagrams and credentials it has become a known-environment test. That isn't wrong in itself, but the scope document must be updated so the results are judged as the right kind of test.`},
  say: `I know which rules apply, keep evidence that controls work, respect privacy rights within the law, and match the audit or test type to the question being asked.`
},
'quiz:sec:Security awareness': {
  why: `People are the first target: phishing, phone scams, tailgating. Awareness training turns staff into early warnings, and the help desk is often where their reports arrive.`,
  idea: `<ul class="clean"><li><b>Phishing</b>: run simulated campaigns, teach the signs (urgency, an odd sender, a link asking you to sign in), and make reporting one click. The security team responds to every report.</li><li><b>Unusual behavior</b>: risky (turning off security tools), unexpected (a sign-in at 3 a.m. from a new country), unintentional (emailing a file to the wrong person).</li><li><b>Topics</b>: password and credential management, removable media and cables (never plug in a found USB stick), social engineering, situational awareness (who is behind you, who followed you in), operational security (don't post photos of badges or screens), insider threats, and hybrid or remote work (screens in public, public Wi-Fi).</li><li><b>Training types</b>: at onboarding, ongoing, targeted at higher-risk groups, and corrective after a mistake.</li><li><b>Measure it</b>: over repeated campaigns, the click rate should fall and the report rate should rise; report the results to managers.</li></ul>`,
  seen: `<pre>Phishing simulation, Q3 2026          sent: 400
Clicked link        28   (7%)     last quarter 13%
Entered password     6   (1.5%)
Reported           132   (33%)    last quarter 18%
Median time to first report: 4 minutes</pre><p>A campaign report: fewer clicks and more reports than last quarter.</p>`,
  example: `<p>An email "from IT" asks staff to re-enter their password. Three people report it within four minutes. The security team confirms it is phishing, blocks the sender and the link for everyone, finds the two people who clicked and resets their passwords. Three reports protected the whole company.</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Punishing people who click: they stop reporting, which is worse.`, `Plugging in a found USB stick to find its owner.`, `Holding a badge door open for someone you don't know.`, `Measuring only the click rate: the report rate shows whether people act.`],
  spot: {q: `After a phishing test, a manager wants to publish the names of everyone who clicked. Good idea?`, a: `No. Shaming makes people hide mistakes and stop reporting. Give those people targeted training, and track click and report rates for the group instead.`},
  say: `I report anything suspicious without engaging with it, never plug in unknown devices, politely stop tailgaters, and judge training by reports as well as clicks.`
},
'quiz:sec:Security tools by job': {
  why: `Job posts and interviews name tools, and Security+ asks which tool fits a task. Knowing what each kind of tool is for lets you pick the right one, even when the brand on the job post is new to you.`,
  idea: `<ul class="clean"><li><b>See traffic</b>: a packet capture tool records the packets on a link (Wireshark, tcpdump).</li><li><b>See logs</b>: a SIEM (security information and event management) system gathers logs from many systems and alerts on patterns (Splunk, Microsoft Sentinel).</li><li><b>Watch the network</b>: an IDS (intrusion detection system) alerts on known-bad traffic; an IPS (intrusion prevention system) can also block it (Snort, Suricata).</li><li><b>Watch each device</b>: EDR (endpoint detection and response) watches what runs on laptops and servers and can isolate one (Microsoft Defender for Endpoint, CrowdStrike Falcon).</li><li><b>Find weaknesses</b>: a vulnerability scanner checks for missing patches and weak settings (Nessus, OpenVAS); a network scanner lists which hosts and ports answer (Nmap).</li><li><b>Test, with permission only</b>: a web testing proxy sits between your browser and an app (Burp Suite, or ZAP (Zed Attack Proxy) from OWASP (Open Worldwide Application Security Project)); a password auditing tool checks how fast stored hashes could be guessed (Hashcat, John the Ripper).</li><li><b>Investigate</b>: a forensic imager makes an exact copy of a disk, and a hash of the copy proves it wasn't changed (FTK Imager, dd); a file reputation lookup checks a hash against many antivirus engines (VirusTotal).</li></ul>`,
  seen: `<pre>Job post: SOC Analyst I
Must have: SIEM experience (Sentinel or Splunk), EDR triage,
           packet analysis (Wireshark), vulnerability scan review (Nessus)</pre><p>Each tool in the list is one job: logs, endpoints, traffic and weaknesses. A security operations center (SOC) analyst uses all four in a normal week.</p>`,
  example: `<p>An alert in the SIEM says a laptop contacted a known-bad address. You open the EDR to see which process made the connection and isolate the laptop. You pull the packet capture to see what was sent, and look up the file's hash on VirusTotal instead of running it.</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Learning tool names without their job: a new product is easy to learn once you know what kind of tool it is.`, `Confusing an IDS (alerts) with an IPS (can also block).`, `Running a scanner, proxy or password tool against systems you don't own or have written permission to test. Authorization comes first, every time.`, `Copying a disk for an investigation without hashing the copy, so you can't prove it wasn't changed.`],
  spot: {q: `A help desk tech downloads a suspicious attachment and double-clicks it in a virtual machine (VM) "to see what it does." What's the safer first step?`, a: `Hash the file and look up the hash on a file reputation service such as VirusTotal, without running it. Detonating malware is a job for an isolated analysis environment and the security team.`},
  say: `I think of tools by their job: SIEM for logs, EDR for endpoints, packet capture for traffic, scanners for weaknesses. So a new product is just a new name for a job I already understand, and testing tools only go on systems I'm authorized to test.`
},
'quiz:sec:Threat actors': {
  why: `Knowing who is likely to attack, and why, tells you what to defend first. A clinic worried about ransomware gangs needs different priorities from a defense contractor facing a government-backed group. Security+ (SY0-801 objective 2.2) asks you to match a situation to its actor and motive.`,
  idea: `<ul class="clean"><li><b>Actors</b>: organized crime (attacks run as a business), hacktivists (a cause), state-sponsored groups (government money and patience), insiders (malicious or accidental), unskilled attackers (ready-made tools), competitors (a business advantage).</li><li><b>Motivations</b>: financial gain, extortion, espionage, revenge, ideology or politics, notoriety, fear and chaos, sometimes plain curiosity.</li><li><b>Attributes</b> that separate them: internal or external, how much money and time they have, and how skilled they are.</li></ul>`,
  seen: `<pre>Threat model: regional hospital          reviewed 2026-11
Most likely   Organized crime (ransomware)    motive: extortion
              Unintentional insider           motive: none (mistakes)
Less likely   Hacktivist                      motive: ideological
Unlikely      State-sponsored                 nothing they need here</pre><p>A short threat model: the defenses go to the actors near the top first.</p>`,
  example: `<p>A ransomware group encrypts a hospital's files and threatens to publish patient records unless it pays. Actor: organized crime. Motive: extortion (the threat), with financial gain as the end goal. A week later a nurse emails a patient list to the wrong address. Actor: an unintentional insider. There was no motive at all, and the fix is training and data loss controls, not threat hunting.</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Calling every attacker a "hacker": the actor type decides which defenses matter.`, `Forgetting insiders: they already have access, and many incidents are mistakes rather than malice.`, `Mixing up extortion (a threat forces the payment) with plain financial gain (the data is sold).`, `Assuming a skilled, patient attack must be a teenager: long, quiet campaigns point to well-funded groups.`],
  spot: {q: `A security plan for a small accounting firm spends most of its budget defending against state-sponsored espionage. What's wrong with the priorities?`, a: `A small firm is far more likely to face organized crime (ransomware, invoice fraud) and insider mistakes. Spend first on backups, multifactor sign-in and staff training; government-backed groups are a lower risk for it.`},
  say: `I start a threat model by asking who would realistically attack us and why. That points the budget at the likely actors, like ransomware groups and insider mistakes, instead of the dramatic ones.`
},
'quiz:sec:Threat vectors': {
  why: `A threat vector is the path an attack takes in. Closing paths is cheaper than cleaning up afterward, and Security+ (SY0-801 objective 2.3) lists them by type: messages, files, browsers, networks, devices, suppliers, people and signals.`,
  idea: `<ul class="clean"><li><b>Message-based</b>: email, SMS (Short Message Service) texts, instant messaging and collaboration tools.</li><li><b>Image and file-based</b>: QR (quick response) codes that hide links; documents with embedded macros.</li><li><b>Browser-based</b>: malicious extensions, stolen cookies and session tokens.</li><li><b>Network and remote access</b>: exposed remote desktop, unpatched VPN gateways, infrastructure devices.</li><li><b>Supply chain</b>: managed service providers, software suppliers and other third parties with access.</li><li><b>Human and physical</b>: impersonation, a malicious USB drive, a watering hole (infecting a site the targets already visit), tailgating past locks.</li><li><b>Built-in tools</b>: living-off-the-land tools such as PowerShell, which leave no malware file behind.</li></ul>`,
  seen: `<pre>Email gateway  2026-11-12 09:14
From:     billing@paypa1-support.com
Subject:  Invoice 4471 overdue
Attach:   Invoice_4471.docm   (macro-enabled)   -> QUARANTINED
Rule:     block Office files with macros from outside senders</pre><p>A file-based vector stopped at the gateway: the <code>.docm</code> extension means the Word file can run macros.</p>`,
  example: `<p>A parking meter carries a sticker with a QR code that leads to a fake payment page. That's an image-based vector (quishing). The fix isn't antivirus: it's teaching people to check the address a code opens before paying, and the city checking its meters. Each vector has its own control.</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Thinking email is the only way in: texts, QR codes, browser add-ons and suppliers are all common vectors.`, `Trusting a managed service provider so completely that its access is never reviewed.`, `Leaving remote desktop open to the internet "just for the weekend."`, `Assuming no malware file means no attack: living-off-the-land attacks use the system's own tools.`],
  spot: {q: `To make support easier, an IT provider has a remote desktop port open to the internet on every client's main server, with one shared password. What vectors does this create?`, a: `Two at once: an exposed remote access service that anyone can try to sign in to, and a supply chain path, because breaking that one shared password reaches every client. Put remote access behind a VPN with multifactor sign-in, and give each client its own credentials.`},
  say: `I map how an attack could get in (messages, files, browsers, remote access, suppliers and people) and close the cheapest paths first, like macros from outside senders and remote desktop on the internet.`
},
'quiz:sec:Vulnerability types': {
  why: `A vulnerability is a weakness an attacker can use. Naming the type tells you the fix: an unpatched system needs the patch, a misconfiguration needs a setting changed, a zero-day needs other layers of defense. Security+ (SY0-801 objectives 2.1 and 2.4) asks you to name the type and to prioritize.`,
  idea: `<ul class="clean"><li><b>Systems</b>: unpatched (the fix exists but isn't installed), unsupported or obsolete (no fixes are coming), unmanaged or rogue devices, shadow IT (systems nobody in IT knows about).</li><li><b>Code</b>: hardcoded secrets, race conditions (a gap between the time-of-check (TOC) and the time-of-use (TOU)), unsafe exception handling that shows internal details, malicious updates.</li><li><b>Settings and storage</b>: misconfiguration, public object storage, public repositories, stale credentials.</li><li><b>Zero-day</b>: a flaw the vendor doesn't know about yet, so no patch exists.</li><li><b>Naming and rating</b>: a CVE (Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures) ID names a flaw; a CVSS (Common Vulnerability Scoring System) score from 0 to 10 rates its severity. Prioritize by severity, likelihood and impact, starting with what is exposed to the internet.</li></ul>`,
  seen: `<pre>Scan summary            2026-11-12
Host          Finding                                   CVSS   Exposure
web-01        CVE-2026-21001 (patch released 2026-05)   9.8    internet
files-02      OS past end of support                    n/a    internal
test-07       CVE-2026-21001                            9.8    isolated lab</pre><p>The same flaw on two hosts: <code>web-01</code> is patched first because it faces the internet. <code>files-02</code> needs a replacement plan, because no patch will ever come.</p>`,
  example: `<p>Same CVE, two servers. The internet-facing web server is patched tonight; the isolated test box waits for the normal cycle. Severity is the same, but exposure, and so likelihood, is not. That is prioritization.</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Treating "unpatched" and "unsupported" as the same: one needs the patch installed, the other needs replacing, because no patch is coming.`, `Calling every unpatched flaw a zero-day: a zero-day has no patch yet.`, `Prioritizing by CVSS score alone and ignoring exposure and business impact.`, `Removing a password from the code but not changing it: it is still in the history and in every copy.`],
  spot: {q: `A developer finds an API key in a public repository, deletes the line and pushes the change. Is the vulnerability fixed?`, a: `No. The key is still in the commit history and in any copy made while it was public. Revoke and rotate the key first, then move secrets out of the code into a secrets manager.`},
  say: `I name the type of weakness first, because that tells me the fix, then prioritize by severity, exposure and business impact rather than the CVSS number alone.`
},
'quiz:sec:Indicators of compromise': {
  why: `Most attacks are caught by spotting their traces: odd sign-ins, cleared logs, unusual load. Security+ (SY0-801 objective 2.5) gives you a scenario and asks which attack or indicator it shows, and analysts do the same thing all day.`,
  idea: `<ul class="clean"><li><b>Sign-in clues</b>: impossible travel (two places too far apart for the time between), concurrent sessions, account lockouts, MFA prompt floods (an MFA bypass attempt).</li><li><b>Credential attacks</b>: brute force (many passwords against one account), password spraying (one password against many accounts, slow enough to dodge lockouts), user enumeration (finding which usernames exist).</li><li><b>System clues</b>: logs cleared or edited (log manipulation), excessive resource consumption, unknown processes, odd file timestamps.</li><li><b>Application attacks</b>: injection, directory traversal (<code>../</code> to leave the web folder), privilege escalation, replay.</li></ul>`,
  seen: `<pre>Sign-in log: j.ortiz
09:02  Chicago, US        success   laptop
09:21  Singapore, SG      success   new device
09:21  MFA push x 37      denied ... approved 03:12 next day</pre><p>Impossible travel, then an MFA prompt flood that finally worked: this account is compromised.</p>`,
  example: `<p>Overnight, 400 accounts each get one failed sign-in using the password "Winter2026!". No single account locks out. That's password spraying. Brute force would hammer one account until it locked. Respond by blocking the source, checking which accounts succeeded, and requiring multifactor sign-in everywhere.</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Mixing up brute force (one account, many passwords) and password spraying (many accounts, one password).`, `Ignoring cleared logs: an empty log where there should be entries is itself evidence.`, `Telling users to approve MFA prompts "to make them stop": that is exactly how a prompt flood gets in.`, `Treating a lockout wave as a help desk problem only: mass lockouts mean someone is guessing passwords.`],
  spot: {q: `A login page answers "No such user" for unknown names and "Incorrect password" for real ones. Why does a security reviewer flag it?`, a: `It allows user enumeration: an attacker can confirm which accounts exist and then target only those. Show the same message for both cases, such as "Incorrect username or password."`},
  say: `I read sign-in and system logs for patterns, like impossible travel, prompt floods, cleared logs and slow password spraying, and I know which attack each pattern points to.`
},
'quiz:sec:AI threats': {
  why: `Companies now use AI (artificial intelligence) assistants and models every day, and attackers target them. SY0-801 added an objective (2.6) for AI-specific threats, and the questions are about recognizing each one.`,
  idea: `<ul class="clean"><li><b>Attacks on the input</b>: prompt injection (instructions hidden in content the model reads, such as a web page or email), jailbreaking (a user talking the model out of its safety rules), evasion (input crafted so a trained detector misses it).</li><li><b>Attacks on the training</b>: poisoning (tampered training data teaches the model the wrong thing), model manipulation.</li><li><b>Risks in normal use</b>: hallucinations (confident, false answers), data loss (sensitive data pasted into an outside tool), privacy, bias (unfair results learned from skewed data), explainability (nobody can say why it decided something).</li><li><b>Controls</b>: treat model output as untrusted input, give AI tools the least access they need, keep sensitive data out of public tools, and have a person review high-stakes decisions.</li></ul>`,
  seen: `<pre>Data loss alert   2026-11-12 14:03
User:        m.chen (Finance)
Destination: public AI chat site
Content:     2,400 rows matching card-number pattern
Action:      upload blocked, user notified, ticket opened</pre><p>A data loss control stopping company data from going into a public AI tool.</p>`,
  example: `<p>An AI assistant that can read email is asked to summarize the inbox. One email contains hidden text telling it to forward the finance folder to an outside address. That is prompt injection. The fix is limiting what the assistant can do (it summarizes, it doesn't send), not trusting it to ignore the text.</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Treating AI output as fact: check citations and figures, because hallucinations sound confident.`, `Pasting customer or company data into a public AI tool.`, `Confusing prompt injection (hidden instructions in content) with jailbreaking (a user arguing the model past its rules).`, `Confusing poisoning (bad training data) with evasion (crafted input to a model that is already trained).`],
  spot: {q: `A team connects an AI assistant to the help desk with full admin rights so it can "fix anything" users ask for. What's the risk?`, a: `Anyone who gets text in front of it, through a ticket or an email, could use prompt injection to make it act as an admin. Give it the least access it needs, and require a person to approve changes.`},
  say: `I treat AI tools like any other system: least access, no sensitive data in public tools, and a person checks anything high-stakes, because prompt injection and hallucinations are real failure modes.`
},
'quiz:sec:Change management': {
  why: `Most outages and many security holes come from changes: a patch, a new firewall rule, a moved server. Change management makes every change reviewed, tested, reversible and written down. SY0-801 objective 1.2 asks you to apply it to a scenario.`,
  idea: `<ul class="clean"><li><b>Before</b>: an owner, the stakeholders, an impact analysis (what could this break?), test results, and approval from the CAB (change advisory board).</li><li><b>When</b>: inside a maintenance window, the agreed time slot that hurts the business least. Plan for downtime and for service or application restarts.</li><li><b>If it fails</b>: a backout plan puts the old version back. Fail forward means pushing a small fix instead, when going back would cost more.</li><li><b>Technical traps</b>: dependencies (one system relies on another), legacy applications that can't be updated, and allow lists/deny lists that must change with the system.</li><li><b>After</b>: update diagrams, policies and procedures, and keep every version of the configuration in version control.</li></ul>`,
  seen: `<pre>CHG-2291  Upgrade VPN appliance firmware 9.4 -> 9.6
Owner: j.ortiz   Window: Sun 02:00-04:00   Status: CAB approved
Impact: remote staff offline about 10 min (service restart)
Tested: staging 11/08 OK   Backout: reload 9.4 image (saved)</pre><p>A change ticket with an owner, a window, the impact, test results and a way back.</p>`,
  example: `<p>A team wants to patch a web server. Impact analysis shows the payment service depends on an old library the patch replaces. The CAB approves the patch only for a Sunday window, with a tested backout plan and a note to update the network diagram after the payment service moves.</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Making an "urgent quick fix" outside the process: emergency changes still get recorded and reviewed afterwards.`, `Having no backout plan, or one that was never tested.`, `Forgetting dependencies: the change works, and something else breaks.`, `Not updating diagrams and procedures, so the next responder works from an old map.`],
  spot: {q: `A firewall rule change was approved and made, but nobody saved the old configuration. The change breaks the VPN (virtual private network) for remote staff. What was missing?`, a: `A backout plan, plus version control of the configuration. With the old version saved, the team could restore it in minutes.`},
  say: `I treat every change as a small project: an owner, an impact analysis, approval, a window, test results and a way back. And I update the documentation afterwards.`
},
'quiz:sec:Architecture models': {
  why: `Where systems run changes who protects what. A server in your own building, a public cloud and a serverless function each put different security work on you. SY0-801 objective 3.1 asks you to compare these models.`,
  idea: `<ul class="clean"><li><b>Cloud deployment models</b>: public (shared provider hardware), private (one organization only), community (shared by organizations with the same needs), hybrid (your own infrastructure plus a cloud). Multicloud means more than one provider.</li><li><b>On-premises infrastructure</b>: you run everything, including power, cooling and physical security.</li><li><b>Serverless and microservices</b>: serverless runs your code on demand with no servers for you to patch; microservices split an app into small separate services. Both shrink what one flaw can reach, and both add many permissions and connections to manage.</li><li><b>IaC (Infrastructure as Code)</b>: infrastructure written as files, so it can be reviewed, repeated and checked for drift.</li><li><b>Separation</b>: logical segmentation (VLANs, or virtual LANs (local area networks), and firewall rules on shared hardware), physical segmentation (separate hardware), and an air-gapped network (no connection at all), common for OT (operational technology).</li><li><b>Trade-offs to weigh</b>: availability, resilience, cost, scalability, ease of recovery, responsibility, and data sovereignty (data falls under the laws of the country where it is stored).</li></ul>`,
  seen: `<pre>resource "aws_s3_bucket" "logs" {
  bucket = "corp-audit-logs"
}
# region: eu-central-1  (data must stay in the EU)</pre><p>Infrastructure as Code, with the region chosen for data sovereignty.</p>`,
  example: `<p>A clinic keeps patient records in its own data center and runs its booking website in a public cloud. That is a hybrid cloud. The clinic still patches and physically guards its own servers, while the provider secures the cloud hardware and the clinic secures its website settings and accounts.</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Thinking "it's in the cloud, so the provider secures it": you still own your data, accounts and settings.`, `Confusing hybrid (your own infrastructure plus a cloud) with multicloud (more than one cloud provider).`, `Treating logical segmentation as an air gap: VLANs on shared switches can still be misconfigured or bypassed.`],
  spot: {q: `A company says its plant's control network is "air-gapped", but an engineer connects to it from home through a remote desktop tool. What's wrong?`, a: `It isn't air-gapped any more. A remote connection is a network path in. Either remove it or treat the network as connected and segment and monitor it.`},
  say: `When I look at an architecture, I ask who is responsible for each layer, how it's separated, and how quickly it can recover. Then I check where the data has to live.`
},
'quiz:sec:Asset management': {
  why: `You can't protect, patch or wipe what you don't know you have. Lost laptops, forgotten servers and drives sold with data still on them are common breach stories. SY0-801 objective 4.2 covers the asset management life cycle.`,
  idea: `<ul class="clean"><li><b>Planning/scoping</b>: decide what is needed and its security requirements before buying.</li><li><b>Acquisition/procurement process</b>: buy only through approved suppliers, and check what arrives.</li><li><b>Assignment/accounting</b>: record every asset against a named owner.</li><li><b>Monitoring/asset tracking</b>: keep the inventory current: where each asset is, what runs on it, whether it is patched. Unknown devices on the network are a warning sign.</li><li><b>Disposal/decommissioning</b>: wipe or destroy data with proof, remove accounts and records, and retire the asset from the inventory.</li></ul>`,
  seen: `<pre>Asset tag   Type     Assigned to   Last check-in   Disk encrypted
LT-0441     Laptop   r.singh       2026-11-11      yes
LT-0457     Laptop   (none)        2026-08-02      no   &lt;-- investigate
SRV-112     Server   IT ops        2026-11-12      yes</pre><p>An inventory that shows an unassigned laptop that hasn't checked in for months.</p>`,
  example: `<p>A company retires an old file server. The team archives the data, removes the server's accounts and DNS (Domain Name System) records, wipes the drives, keeps a certificate of destruction, and marks the asset as disposed in the inventory.</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Deleting files or reformatting before disposal: the data can often still be recovered.`, `Keeping the inventory in a spreadsheet nobody updates.`, `Forgetting software and data assets: licenses, cloud accounts and databases have life cycles too.`],
  spot: {q: `A network scan finds 14 devices that aren't in the inventory. Which stage of asset management failed, and what's the risk?`, a: `Monitoring/asset tracking. Unknown devices aren't patched or watched, and some may be rogue devices an attacker plugged in.`},
  say: `I think of every asset as having a life: planned, bought, assigned, tracked, then safely retired. Most incidents I'd worry about happen in the gaps between those steps.`
},
'quiz:sec:Automation & orchestration': {
  why: `Security teams can't do everything by hand. Automation creates accounts, builds servers, opens tickets and fixes drift in seconds, the same way every time. It can also make mistakes at the same speed, so SY0-801 objective 4.6 asks about its uses, risks and AI (artificial intelligence) capabilities.`,
  idea: `<ul class="clean"><li><b>Use cases</b>: user provisioning, resource provisioning, desired state management (keep systems in their approved state), anomaly detection, and ticket management.</li><li><b>Considerations</b>: guardrails (limits on what automation may do), automation logic, complexity, cost, process risks, and deployment.</li><li><b>AI capabilities</b>: agentic AI (acts on its own), chatbots (answer questions), predictive analysis (forecasts what is likely next), and AI-augmented baselines (AI keeps the picture of normal up to date).</li><li><b>SecOps (security operations)</b>: continuous integration (CI) and continuous deployment (CD) pipelines, together called CI/CD, run security checks on every code change.</li><li><b>Outcomes</b>: time saved, baselines enforced, reduced downtime, and a team that acts earlier.</li></ul>`,
  seen: `<pre>[playbook] new-hire.yml   trigger: HR status = started
  create account  a.lee      groups: sales-users
  license         email      OK
  ticket          INC-5521   closed (auto)</pre><p>User provisioning and ticket management in one automated run.</p>`,
  example: `<p>A script disables accounts that haven't signed in for 90 days. A guardrail limits it to 20 accounts per run and skips admin and service accounts, so a bad input can't lock out the whole company.</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Automating without guardrails: a bug runs on every system at once.`, `Giving agentic AI broad rights with no person reviewing its actions.`, `Automating a broken process: it just fails faster. Fix the process first.`],
  spot: {q: `An AI tool is allowed to isolate any laptop it suspects, with no limits. One morning it isolates the whole sales team. What was missing?`, a: `Guardrails: limits on how many devices it can act on, and a person approving actions above that.`},
  say: `I automate repeatable work like provisioning, tickets and baseline checks, and I always add guardrails and logging, because automation makes mistakes as fast as it fixes them.`
},
'quiz:sec:Acronyms': {
  why: `Security+ questions, job posts and tickets are full of acronyms, and the exam won't spell them out. CompTIA's SY0-801 objectives end with an official acronym list and ask candidates to know all of it. If an acronym stops you mid-question, you lose time and often the point.`,
  idea: `<ul class="clean"><li><b>Know the words</b>: RTO (recovery time objective) is easier to keep apart from RPO (recovery point objective) once the words are clear: one is about time down, the other about data lost.</li><li><b>Know the job</b>: the words alone aren't enough. Say what it does in one line.</li><li><b>Learn lookalikes together</b>: SPF (Sender Policy Framework), DKIM (DomainKeys Identified Mail) and DMARC (Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting, and Conformance) are three parts of one email-trust system, and the exam likes to mix them up.</li></ul>`,
  seen: `<pre>Ticket 4471: CAB approved. Update the SPF record, then check
DMARC reports for a week. RPO for the mail archive stays 24h.</pre><p>One short ticket, four acronyms: the CAB (change advisory board) approved the change, SPF and DMARC are email checks, and RPO is how much data you may lose.</p>`,
  example: `<p>An exam question says a company can be down for 4 hours but can lose only 15 minutes of data. The 4 hours is the RTO, and the 15 minutes is the RPO. Knowing the words "time" and "point" answers it without guessing.</p>`,
  mistakes: [`Learning the letters without the job: you can spell out SIEM (security information and event management) and still miss a question that describes what it does.`, `Mixing up detect and prevent pairs: an IDS (intrusion detection system) only alerts; an IPS (intrusion prevention system) can block.`, `Cramming the whole list in one sitting. Short daily rounds, spread over days, stick far better.`],
  spot: {q: `A question says a battery unit kept the servers running during a short power cut. Is that an RPS (redundant power supply) or a UPS (uninterruptible power supply)?`, a: `The UPS. An RPS is a second power supply inside the same device, which doesn't help if the building loses power.`},
  say: `I learn acronyms as words plus a job, and I study the lookalikes side by side, like RPO and RTO, or IDS and IPS. That way I can follow a ticket or an exam question without stopping.`
}
});

